Subject: standard: fflush() makes written data visible on OpenVMS

On OpenVMS, data written to a file is seen by other opens of it and by
stat() only after fsync() or close: until then the file reads as empty
(probe r_file_sem: size 0, a second open reads 0 bytes; after fsync() 36).
PHP's fflush() is a no-op for unbuffered plain files, so code that writes,
fflush()es and reads the file back, or stats it, saw nothing (vms-php
PORTING_LOG #39; fflush_basic, ftruncate_error).  On VMS fflush() also
fsync()s a plain file stream that is a regular file.  Reads without a
flush still see nothing until close; flushing on every write would cost a
disk write each time.
---
--- a/ext/standard/file.c
+++ b/ext/standard/file.c
@@ -1019,6 +1019,19 @@
 	if (ret) {
 		RETURN_FALSE;
 	}
+#ifdef __VMS
+	/* On OpenVMS data written to a file is seen by other opens and stat()
+	 * only after fsync() or close: fflush() makes it visible, as on POSIX. */
+	if (php_stream_is(stream, PHP_STREAM_IS_STDIO)) {
+		int fd;
+		zend_stat_t sb;
+
+		if (php_stream_cast(stream, PHP_STREAM_AS_FD, (void **) &fd, 0) == SUCCESS
+				&& zend_fstat(fd, &sb) == 0 && S_ISREG(sb.st_mode)) {
+			fsync(fd);
+		}
+	}
+#endif
 	RETURN_TRUE;
 }
 /* }}} */
